--expect
0 1111111122230
--expect-why
DERIVED FROM THE `case` IN `eq.kin` AGAINST THE FIXTURE'S TAG NUMBERS. The
probe's `ty` is the identity on the heap address's low byte, so `Value(0x100|h)`
has type tag `h`, and the tags are numbered in the head:

  1 CONS  2 EMPTY_LIST  3 LAZYSEQ  4 VECSEQ  5 STRSEQ  6 RANGE
  7 VEC   8 MAPENTRY    9 ARRAYMAP 10 HASHMAP 11 TABLEREF 12 SET

and the categories SCALAR 0, SEQUENTIAL 1, MAP 2, SET 3.

  Value(7)   not on the heap          -> 0   (the leading field)
  tags 1..8  the SEQUENTIAL list      -> 11111111
  tags 9..11 the MAP list             -> 222
  tag 12     SET                      -> 3
  tag 13     named by no arm          -> 0   (the `:else`)

THE TWO ZEROS ARE THE LOAD-BEARING FIELDS, at opposite ends of the line. The
leading one says a non-heap value never reaches the tag read at all; the
trailing one says an UNKNOWN tag falls to SCALAR rather than to whichever arm
happens to be last. Everything between them is a membership list, and this is
a codebase whose first rule is that two tables agreeing on the common cases
are indistinguishable from one table until an uncommon case arrives -- so the
whole line is that list, written down where a change to `category` has to
change it.

`11` AT POSITION 8 IS `TY_MAPENTRY`, and it is SEQUENTIAL, not MAP. A map
entry is a pair, and `=` compares it with a two-element vector.

`2` AT POSITION 11 IS `TY_TABLEREF`, and that is the one this file exists to
say: A ROW REF IS IN THE MAP CATEGORY, because it is `=` to a map with the
same entries (`DECISIONS.md#tables`). Nothing else in the tree states it; if
it moved to SCALAR, row refs would silently stop comparing equal to maps and
this field is what would notice.
